WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.751
Working with a remote. Your commits stay on your laptop until you say so.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:14.963
Ana has a project on her laptop. Another copy lives on GitHub. Git never syncs them by itself.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.628
The copy on GitHub has a name: origin.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:25.615
She creates a branch, validate-form, and makes a commit. It exists only on her laptop.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:30.385
Reviewers and deployment tools read GitHub, not her laptop.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:38.622
To share it, she pushes. The first time, she adds -u, so Git remembers the link.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:44.538
Commits fly up. Only commits: not files, not edits.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.756
On GitHub, the branch validate-form now exists.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.933
Her laptop also keeps a picture of the remote: origin/main.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.799
It's a ghost label. It moves only when she talks to GitHub.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:05.017
So when Luis pushes to main, her picture is already out of date.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:13.920
git status says: up to date with origin/main. That means the picture. Not GitHub, live.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.435
git fetch asks GitHub what's new.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:25.938
Luis's commits fly down. Only the ghost label moves. Her branch and her files don't change.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.156
git log origin/main shows what arrived.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:35.455
Now she can look first. A fetch can never cause a conflict.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.737
git pull is a fetch, followed by a merge into your current branch.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:49.308
The ghost label moves, and then main catches up. If she had no new commits, it's a fast-forward.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:55.015
If both sides have new commits, pull makes a merge commit, and it may conflict.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.732
Her habit: pull before you start, and pull before you push.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:08.212
Skip it, and her next push is rejected: fetch first. Never force it. Pull, then push again.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.556
Back on validate-form, her branch is on GitHub. Now she opens a pull request.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:19.881
She says what changed and why, and asks Luis to review.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:26.586
It's a page that says: please merge this branch into main. It shows the diff.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:34.933
Luis reads it and leaves a comment. Ana fixes it and pushes to the same branch. The pull request updates.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.187
Luis approves.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:44.647
She clicks Merge pull request. On GitHub, validate-form joins main.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.859
Her laptop doesn't know yet. She switches to main, and pulls.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:57.132
Now both copies agree. She deletes the branch with git branch -d, and starts the next one.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:02.287
Keep each pull request to one change, and merge it within a day or two.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.067
Practise it with the commit and branch simulator in Version control.
